course: dictionary
lesson: 144
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words wear different coats. The same word can mean different things in different sentences, and the words around it tell you which meaning to use. «The affair was soon over» and «foreign affairs» both use affair, but they do not mean the same thing.
explain: In «The party was a noisy affair», affair means an event or happening. In «The Senate controlled money and foreign affairs», affairs means matters, the business that countries deal with. Ask yourself: what does the sentence seem to be about?
explain: Try swapping in a meaning. «The affair was soon over» still makes sense if you say «The event was soon over», so event is a good fit. Swapping meanings is a quick check, just as it is with any new word.
fact: An affair is an event or happening, such as a party or a big occasion.
fact: Affairs (in the plural) means matters or business, such as money affairs or world affairs.
fact: «That's my affair» means it is my business, not yours.
fact: Some grown-up stories use affair for a secret romance between two people, when one or both are married to someone else. You will meet this later.
fact: Phrases like «current affairs» and «state of affairs» use the business meaning, and the words around them show it.
exercise: «The party was a noisy affair.» What does affair mean here? | A quiet rest | *An event or happening | A secret romance | A pile of papers
exercise: Dad says, «What I do in my spare time is my affair.» What does he mean? | It is a party for my family | It is a secret I must tell everyone | *It is my business, not yours | It is my favourite game
exercise: «The Senate controlled money and foreign affairs.» What do foreign affairs mean? | *Matters between countries and their leaders | Words spoken in another language | Strange animals from far away | A trip to a foreign country on holiday
test: Which phrase means the situation or state of things right now? | *state of affairs | running away fast | quiet park | something you own
test: Tom says affair always means a secret romance. Is he right? | *No, it can also mean an event or matters of business | Yes, always | Only when it is written with a capital letter | Yes, but only in news reports
exam: Which word could replace affair in «The party was a noisy affair» without changing the meaning? | secret | business | *event | quarrel
